---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "bare performance nutrition protein powder",
      "bpn protein powder",
      "bpn whey protein review",
      "bare performance nutrition whey",
      "is bpn protein third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Bare Performance Nutrition Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Nick Bare's supplement brand sells three protein powders with NSF Certified for Sport claims. We check the labels, the testing, the sweeteners and what each costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-bpn">Who Makes BPN</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in BPN Whey</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#testing">Is BPN Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which BPN Protein to Buy</a>
      <a href="#compare">How BPN Compares</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip BPN</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Bare Performance Nutrition (BPN) Whey is a well-documented whey and casein blend: 25 g of protein in a 34.1 g scoop, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit, and enrolled in NSF's Certified for Sport program, per the brand. At the $54.99 list price for 27 servings it costs about $2.04 per 25 g of protein, which is expensive, but BPN's frequent 30 percent sales bring it down to about $1.43. It is a good buy for tested athletes and for anyone who wants a certified whey without sucralose, as long as they buy it on sale.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. BPN Protein Powder: The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            BPN Whey is one of the better-documented wheys an ordinary buyer can get: 25 g of protein per scoop, a short label sweetened with stevia and monk fruit, and enrollment in NSF Certified for Sport and NSF Contents Certified, per the brand. Those are the two things trust-minded buyers ask about most, and BPN answers both. The weakness is price. At $54.99 for 27 servings it is about $2.04 per 25 g of protein, roughly double what a big tub of a certified mainstream whey costs.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">BPN powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">List price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g (list / sale)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein (27 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia, monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$54.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.04 / $1.43</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grass-Fed Whey Isolate (25 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia (Reb M)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$64.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.60 / $1.82</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vegan Protein (27 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia, monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$49.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.31 / $1.62</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices are from BPN's site in November 2026, where every protein showed a sale or Subscribe and Save price about 30 percent below list. BPN discounts often, so the sale column is the realistic one for a patient buyer. Label figures are per the brand's product pages and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict, based on the labels, the brand's testing claims and the prices, not on tasting it ourselves: buy BPN Whey on sale if you are drug-tested or want a certified whey without sucralose. Buy the isolate if lactose bothers you. Skip BPN at full price if all you want is protein per dollar, because Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard and Costco's Kirkland whey cost far less per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to see BPN against the field, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings compare it with the other wheys we track on label, testing and price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-bpn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Bare Performance Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bare Performance Nutrition was founded by Nick Bare, a US Army veteran and endurance athlete who built a large following on YouTube documenting his training for marathons, ultramarathons and strength work. That makes BPN an influencer brand in the plain sense: much of its audience found it through one person's content. The brand is based in Texas, and its products sell through its own site and Texas grocers H-E-B and Central Market, among other retailers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Influencer brands raise a fair trust question, because popularity can run ahead of quality control. BPN's answer is third-party testing. Its product pages state that its supplements are enrolled in NSF Certified for Sport, which screens for banned substances and checks that the contents match the label, and in NSF's Contents Certified program, which BPN says covers formulation, label claims and contaminants such as heavy metals and pesticides. That is more independent checking than most brands of any size publicly claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein range is small and focused. There is the flagship Whey Protein, a Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate, a Vegan Protein and a Collagen Protein, plus seasonal flavors such as a Gingerbread Cookie whey concentrate. BPN also sells pre-workouts, electrolytes, creatine and other supplements; this review covers the protein powders only.
          </p>

          <p>
            BPN is not the only influencer-led protein we have reviewed. Our reviews of <a href="/ryse-protein-powder-review/">Ryse</a>, <a href="/cbum-protein-powder-review/">CBUM</a> and <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost</a> cover brands with a similar audience, and the trust question for each comes down to the same thing: what has been independently tested, and what is just a claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in BPN Whey Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            BPN Whey is a whey and casein blend with 25 g of protein in a 34.1 g scoop, per the brand. That makes it about 73 percent protein by weight, which is typical for a whey that is not a pure isolate. The brand describes the whey as fast-digesting and the casein as slow-digesting, and the blend is what gives the shake a thicker, more milkshake-like body than a straight whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners on the current product page are stevia and monk fruit, and BPN describes the whey as naturally sweetened. That is a meaningful difference from most mainstream wheys, which use sucralose and acesulfame potassium. One caveat: an older retailer listing for the Blueberry Muffin flavor listed sucralose and natural and artificial flavor alongside whey concentrate and micellar casein. Formulas change, and retailers sometimes show old labels, so read the panel on the tub you buy if you avoid sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            BPN's page describes the protein as whey plus casein without giving a ratio, though one retailer listing describes an 88 percent whey and 12 percent casein split. The NSF database entry we found for the vanilla gives a 932 g tub with a 34.5 g serving and 25 g of protein, close to the brand page. The page lists milk as the allergen, the product is not suitable for anyone with a milk allergy, and BPN posts a California Proposition 65 warning for lead on the page, which we discuss in the testing section.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is the leaner product. Per the brand, a scoop gives 25 g of protein, 120 calories, 3 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of fat, from whey isolate described as 95 percent grass-fed Irish whey, from cows that graze an average of 250 or more days a year. The ingredient list is whey isolate, MCT powder, natural flavor, salt and steviol glycosides (Reb M), with cocoa in the chocolate. If lactose upsets you, the isolate is the BPN to choose; our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains why.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A short, naturally sweetened label with 25 g of protein per scoop.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">BPN Whey is a well-built everyday whey. Its label is cleaner than most mass-market wheys; its protein per gram of powder is average for a blend.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The BPN Protein Lineup
          </h2>

          <p>
            BPN sells three main protein powders, plus a collagen powder that we leave out because collagen is not a complete protein. Prices are from bareperformancenutrition.com in November 2026, where the brand showed both a regular price and a Subscribe and Save or sale price; retailer prices from H-E-B and Central Market are noted where we found them. Label figures are per the brand's pages at the time of writing and vary by flavor. The last card is the alternative we would point a price-focused buyer to.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is BPN Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, by the brand's account and by NSF's database. BPN's whey, isolate and vegan pages all state NSF Certified for Sport, and listings for several BPN whey flavors, including Vanilla, Strawberry and S'mores, appeared in NSF's certified products database when we searched in November 2026. NSF Certified for Sport tests for a long list of substances banned in sport, checks that the product contains what the label says, and audits the manufacturing facility. It is the certification most US pro leagues recognize.
          </p>

          <p>
            BPN also says all its products are enrolled in NSF's Contents Certified program, which it describes as testing formulation, label claims and contaminants including heavy metals and pesticides. We could not find published lot-by-lot heavy metal numbers on BPN's pages, so what you get is a certification that an outside body checks these things, rather than a results table you can read. That is still more than most brands provide.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is some history to be aware of. A third-party database page listed BPN whey as Informed Sport certified from 2021 with a later withdrawal date, and an older post on BPN's blog refers to batch certification through a different program. Brands do move between certification programs, and BPN's current pages cite NSF. If certification matters to you, search the NSF Certified for Sport database for the exact flavor and check the lot number on your tub, which is the only way to be sure for any brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Proposition 65 lead warning on BPN's whey page is worth understanding rather than fearing. California requires the warning when a daily serving could exceed a very low lead threshold, and many protein powders, especially chocolate and plant flavors, carry it. It does not mean the product is unsafe at normal use, but if you are pregnant or worried about heavy metals, talk to your doctor, and see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide. This is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does BPN Protein Cost per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At list price, BPN Whey costs about $2.04 per 25 g of protein; on sale, about $1.43. The sum: 27 servings of 25 g is 675 g of protein. $54.99 divided by 675, times 25, is $2.04. At the $38.49 sale price it is $1.43, and at H-E-B's $51.99 shelf price it is about $1.93.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder and price</th>
