        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is BPN protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. BPN states that its whey, isolate and vegan proteins are NSF Certified for Sport, and listings for several BPN whey flavors appeared in NSF's certified products database when we searched in November 2026. BPN also says its products are enrolled in NSF Contents Certified, which covers label claims and contaminants. Check your flavor and lot in NSF's database.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is BPN protein healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label, it is one of the cleaner mainstream wheys: whey and casein with stevia and monk fruit, and no sucralose on the current page. It carries a Proposition 65 lead warning, which many protein powders do. Clean is a marketing word; the NSF certification is the more meaningful signal.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does BPN whey have casein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. BPN Whey Protein is a blend of whey and casein, which the brand describes as fast and slow digesting proteins; one retailer describes the split as 88 percent whey and 12 percent casein. The Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is pure whey isolate with no casein listed.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener is in BPN protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Stevia and monk fruit in the whey and vegan powders, and a stevia extract (Reb M) in the isolate, per BPN's current pages. An older retailer label for one flavor listed sucralose, so if you avoid it, check the panel on your tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which BPN protein flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  BPN does not publish flavor rankings, and we have not tasted them. Reviews on BPN's own site are positive across flavors but are a curated sample. Vanilla and Fudge Chocolate are the safest picks if you also mix protein into oats or coffee; the dessert flavors such as Cinnamon Roll suit straight shakes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in BPN whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  25 g per 34.1 g scoop, per BPN's label, with 27 servings per tub. The isolate also gives 25 g per scoop with 25 servings, and the vegan powder gives 20 g per scoop. Figures can vary slightly by flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where can I buy BPN protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  From bareperformancenutrition.com, which runs frequent sales and Subscribe and Save, and from retailers including H-E-B and Central Market in Texas, where we saw the vanilla whey at $51.99. Vitacost and other online retailers have also listed BPN products.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
